<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kushinagar</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kusinara</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kushinagar, ancient Kusinara, is a town in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, where according to Buddhist tradition the Buddha died and entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parinirvana">parinirvana</a>. It was a centre of the Malla people, who cremated his body and shared out his relics. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lumbini">Lumbini</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodh-gaya">Bodh Gaya</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarnath">Sarnath</a> it is one of the four main places of Buddhist pilgrimage, and its Mahaparinirvana Temple holds a large reclining Buddha.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kushinagar.jpg" alt="Kushinagar" width="320" height="200" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: myself · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kushinara1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Kushinagar, in Pali Kusinārā and in Sanskrit Kuśinagara, is a town in the Kushinagar district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a>. Alexander Cunningham held that the name came from the abundance of kusha grass in the region. According to the Mahāparinibbāṇa Sutta (Sutta 16 of the Dīgha Nikāya), the Buddha in his eightieth year travelled with disciples for months from Rājagṛha through Pāṭaliputta, Vesāli, Bhoganagara and Pāvā to Kushinagar. At Pāvā a resident named Cunda invited them to a meal that included sūkaramaddava, and soon afterwards the Buddha fell ill with a painful complaint resembling dysentery. He crossed the Kakkuttha River, now the Khanua, and reached Kushinagar, where he lay down between sala trees on his right side and attained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parinirvana">parinirvana</a>. His body was cremated seven days later.</p><p>The Sakya clan was to receive the ashes, but six other clans and a king claimed them too. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmin</a> named Drona divided them into eight portions: for Ajātasattu of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/licchavi">Licchavis</a> of Vesāli, the Sakyas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kapilavastu">Kapilavastu</a>, the Bulis of Allakappa, the Koliyas of Rāmagāma, the brahmin of Veṭhadīpa, the Mallas of Pāvā and the Malla king of Kushinagar. According to Buddhaghosa each portion was placed in a reliquary and buried in a tumulus, later enlarged into stupas; of these only the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramagrama">Ramagrama</a> stupa in Ramgram, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, is said to remain intact.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The ruins were first mentioned in modern literature in 1837 by D. Liston, who noted that they were already a place of worship. Alexander Cunningham excavated the Matha Kuar shrine and the Ramabhar stupa in 1861–1862 and was the first archaeologist to identify the site as the place of the Buddha's parinirvana. In 1876 Archibald Carlleyle uncovered the Mahaparinirvana stupa and a reclining Buddha 6.1 metres long. A Burmese monk, Sayadaw U Chandramani, asked the English Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1901 for permission for pilgrims to worship the image. J. Ph. Vogel conducted campaigns between 1904 and 1907. Laurence Waddell suggested in 1896 that the death took place near Rampurva, but archaeology and the sutta both support Kushinagar.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The red sandstone reclining Buddha lies in the Parinirvana Temple, on its right side with its head to the north. The Parinirvana Stupa stands behind it, and the Ramabhar stupa, 1.5 km east, marks the cremation place. The Matha Kuar shrine holds a seated Buddha in blue stone, with an inscription of the 10th or 11th century. Modern temples in Thai and other Buddhist styles also stand there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kushinagar” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He died at <b>Kushinagar</b> at about eighty years of age.</blockquote><a href="/religion/buddhism">Buddhism · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>They were divided into two branches, centred on <b>Kushinagar</b> and Pava.</blockquote><a href="/state/malla-republic">Malla republic</a></li></ul></section>
